</math> must also be multiplied by <math alttext="17"><mn>17</mn>
</math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"61b87506","external_id":"27dab70d-b227-496d-9793-7bbb537c6638","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"11a94d16-ddcf-4f87-b385-eabee90e73c7","uId":"94146940-9197-47af-87d9-9c73971b224b","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.A.","skill_desc":"Ratios, rates, proportional relationships, and units","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">For the values <math alttext=\"j\"><mi>j</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math>, the ratio of <math alttext=\"j\"><mi>j</mi>\n</math> to <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"11\"><mn>11</mn>\n</math> to <math alttext=\"12\"><mn>12</mn>\n</math>. If <math alttext=\"j\"><mi>j</mi>\n</math> is multiplied by <math alttext=\"17\"><mn>17</mn>\n</math>, what is <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math> multiplied by in order to maintain the same ratio?</p>","answerOptions":[],"keys":["17"],"correct_answer":["17"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"17\"><mn>17</mn>\n</math>. If one value is multiplied by a number, then the other value must be multiplied by the same number in order to maintain the same ratio. It&rsquo;s given that <math alttext=\"j\"><mi>j</mi>\n</math> is multiplied by <math alttext=\"17\"><mn>17</mn>\n</math>. Therefore, in order to maintain the same ratio, <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math> must also be multiplied by <math alttext=\"17\"><mn>17</mn>\n</math>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":"manifold","position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959831,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"61b87506","skill_cd":"Q.A.","score_band_range_cd":5,"skill_desc":"Ratios, rates, proportional relationships, and units","createDate":1691007959831,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":null,"external_id":"27dab70d-b227-496d-9793-7bbb537c6638","primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"94146940-9197-47af-87d9-9c73971b224b","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"keys":["17"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"17\"><mn>17</mn>\n</math>. If one value is multiplied by a number, then the other value must be multiplied by the same number in order to maintain the same ratio. It&rsquo;s given that <math alttext=\"j\"><mi>j</mi>\n</math> is multiplied by <math alttext=\"17\"><mn>17</mn>\n</math>. Therefore, in order to maintain the same ratio, <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math> must also be multiplied by <math alttext=\"17\"><mn>17</mn>\n</math>.</p>","origin":"manifold","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">For the values <math alttext=\"j\"><mi>j</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math>, the ratio of <math alttext=\"j\"><mi>j</mi>\n</math> to <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"11\"><mn>11</mn>\n</math> to <math alttext=\"12\"><mn>12</mn>\n</math>. If <math alttext=\"j\"><mi>j</mi>\n</math> is multiplied by <math alttext=\"17\"><mn>17</mn>\n</math>, what is <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math> multiplied by in order to maintain the same ratio?</p>","externalid":"27dab70d-b227-496d-9793-7bbb537c6638","templateid":"2c49ba56-78d9-415a-8975-839d0a683157","vaultid":"11a94d16-ddcf-4f87-b385-eabee90e73c7","type":"spr","answerOptions":[],"correct_answer":["17"]},"createDate":1691007959831,"updateDate":1691007959831}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959831, 1691007959831),
    ($SATQ$61f61789$SATQ$, $SATQ$c084b0a0-2744-443d-9e6a-f230e22fdc88$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Ratios, rates, proportional relationships, and units$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">To study the moisture content in a group of trees, samples from the trunk of each tree were taken from <math alttext="25"><mn>25</mn>
</math> trees and cut in the shape of a cube. The length of the edge of one of these cubes is <math alttext="2.00"><mn>2.00</mn>
</math> centimeters. If this cube has a mass of <math alttext="2.56"><mn>2.56</mn>
</math> grams, what is the density of this cube, in grams per cubic centimeter?</p>$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$["0.32","8/25"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["0.32","8/25"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The correct answer is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext=".32"><mn>.32</mn></math>. The volume of a cube is given by the formula <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper V equals s cubed"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math>, where <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="s"><mi>s</mi></math> is the length of an edge of the cube. It&rsquo;s given that each edge of the cube has a length of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="2.00"><mn>2.00</mn></math> centimeters. Substituting <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="2.00"><mn>2.00</mn></math> centimeters&nbsp;for <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="s"><mi>s</mi></math> in the formula <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper V equals s cubed"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math> yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper V equals left parenthesis 2.00 centimeters right parenthesis cubed"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>2.00</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>centimeters</mtext><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper V equals 8.00"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8.00</mn></math> cubic centimeters. It&rsquo;s given that the cube has a mass of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="2.56"><mn>2.56</mn></math> grams. Dividing the mass, in grams, of the cube by the volume, in cubic centimeters, of the cube gives its density, in grams per cubic centimeters. Therefore, the density of the cube is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="StartFraction 2.56 grams Over 8.00 cubic centimeters EndFraction"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2.56</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>grams</mtext></mrow><mrow><mn>8.00</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>cubic</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>centimeters</mtext></mrow></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext=".32"><mn>.32</mn></math> grams per cubic centimeter. Note that .32 and <span style="font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;">8/25 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</span></p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"61f61789","external_id":"c084b0a0-2744-443d-9e6a-f230e22fdc88","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"9506b820-fe40-4de2-a321-a9aa10d627b7","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.A.","skill_desc":"Ratios, rates, proportional relationships, and units","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">To study the moisture content in a group of trees, samples from the trunk of each tree were taken from <math alttext=\"25\"><mn>25</mn>\n</math> trees and cut in the shape of a cube. The length of the edge of one of these cubes is <math alttext=\"2.00\"><mn>2.00</mn>\n</math> centimeters. If this cube has a mass of <math alttext=\"2.56\"><mn>2.56</mn>\n</math> grams, what is the density of this cube, in grams per cubic centimeter?</p>","answerOptions":null,"keys":["0.32","8/25"],"correct_answer":["0.32","8/25"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\".32\"><mn>.32</mn></math>. The volume of a cube is given by the formula <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper V equals s cubed\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/Ma