/span></p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1755117017723,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"61f61789","skill_cd":"Q.A.","score_band_range_cd":6,"skill_desc":"Ratios, rates, proportional relationships, and units","createDate":1755117017723,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":"","external_id":"c084b0a0-2744-443d-9e6a-f230e22fdc88","primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"9506b820-fe40-4de2-a321-a9aa10d627b7","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"type":"spr","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">To study the moisture content in a group of trees, samples from the trunk of each tree were taken from <math alttext=\"25\"><mn>25</mn>\n</math> trees and cut in the shape of a cube. The length of the edge of one of these cubes is <math alttext=\"2.00\"><mn>2.00</mn>\n</math> centimeters. If this cube has a mass of <math alttext=\"2.56\"><mn>2.56</mn>\n</math> grams, what is the density of this cube, in grams per cubic centimeter?</p>","keys":["0.32","8/25"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\".32\"><mn>.32</mn></math>. The volume of a cube is given by the formula <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper V equals s cubed\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"s\"><mi>s</mi></math> is the length of an edge of the cube. It&rsquo;s given that each edge of the cube has a length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2.00\"><mn>2.00</mn></math> centimeters. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2.00\"><mn>2.00</mn></math> centimeters&nbsp;for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"s\"><mi>s</mi></math> in the formula <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper V equals s cubed\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper V equals left parenthesis 2.00 centimeters right parenthesis cubed\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>2.00</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>centimeters</mtext><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper V equals 8.00\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8.00</mn></math> cubic centimeters. It&rsquo;s given that the cube has a mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2.56\"><mn>2.56</mn></math> grams. Dividing the mass, in grams, of the cube by the volume, in cubic centimeters, of the cube gives its density, in grams per cubic centimeters. Therefore, the density of the cube is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction 2.56 grams Over 8.00 cubic centimeters EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2.56</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>grams</mtext></mrow><mrow><mn>8.00</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>cubic</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>centimeters</mtext></mrow></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\".32\"><mn>.32</mn></math> grams per cubic centimeter. Note that .32 and <span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">8/25 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</span></p>","externalid":"c084b0a0-2744-443d-9e6a-f230e22fdc88","correct_answer":["0.32","8/25"]},"createDate":1755117017723,"updateDate":1755117017723}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117017723, 1755117017723),
    ($SATQ$623dbebb$SATQ$, $SATQ$1edfed1c-89c5-4702-a448-e023544a67b4$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.B.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Percentages$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">A reseller buys certain books for a purchase price of <math alttext="5.00"><mn>5.00</mn>
</math> dollars each and then marks them each for sale at a consumer price that is <math alttext="270 percent sign"><mn>270</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the purchase price. After <math alttext="4"><mn>4</mn>
</math> months, any remaining books not yet sold are marked at a discounted price that is <math alttext="70 percent sign"><mn>70</mn><mo>%</mo></math> off the consumer price. What is the discounted price of each of the remaining books, in dollars?</p>$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$["4.05","81/20"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4.05","81/20"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The correct answer is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="4.05"><mn>4.05</mn></math>. It's given that the purchase price for certain books is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="5.00"><mn>5.00</mn></math> dollars each. It's also given that each book is marked for sale at a consumer price that is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="270 percent sign"><mn>270</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the purchase price. Since the consumer price is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="270 percent sign"><mn>270</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the purchase price of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="5.00"><mn>5.00</mn></math> dollars, it follows that the consumer price is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis 2.7 right parenthesis left parenthesis 5.00 right parenthesis"><mo>(</mo><mn>2.7</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>5.00</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="13.50"><mn>13.50</mn></math>, dollars. It's given that after <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="4"><mn>4</mn></math> months, any remaining books are discounted at <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="70 percent sign"><mn>70</mn><mo>%</mo></math> off the consumer price. Thus, the discount amount is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis 0.7 right parenthesis left parenthesis 13.50 right parenthesis"><mo>(</mo><mn>0.7</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>13.50</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="9.45"><mn>9.45</mn></math>, dollars. Subtracting the discount amount from the consumer price gives the discounted price of each of the remaining books: <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="13.50 minus 9.45 equals 4.05"><mn>13.50</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>9.45</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>4.05</mn></math>. Note that 4.05 and 81/20 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"623dbebb","external_id":"1edfed1c-89c5-4702-a448-e023544a67b4","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"bb812156-24d1-49a9-af95-8b7f023917b6","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.B.","skill_desc":"Percentages","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A reseller buys certain books for a purchase price of <math alttext=\"5.00\"><mn>5.00</mn>\n</math> dollars each and then marks them each for sale at a consumer price that is <math alttext=\"270 percent sign\"><mn>270</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the purchase price. After <math alttext=\"4\"><mn>4</mn>\n</math> months, any remaining books not yet sold are marked at a discounted price that is <math alttext=\"70 percent sign\"><mn>70</mn><mo>%</mo></math> off the consumer price. What is the discounted price of each of the remaining books, in dollars?</p>","answerOptions":null,"keys":["4.05","81/20"],"correct_answer":["4.05","81/20"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"4.05\"><mn>4.05</mn></math>. It's given that the purchase price for certain books is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"5.00\"><mn>5.00</mn></math> dollars each. It's also given that each book is marked for sale at a consumer price that is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"270 percent sign\"><mn>270</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the purchase price. Since the consumer price is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"270 percent sign\"><mn>270</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the purchase price of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"5.00\"><mn>5.00</mn></math> dollars, it follows that the consumer price is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 2.7 right parenthesis left parenthesis 5.00 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mn>2.7</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>5.00</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"13.50\"><mn>13.50</mn></math>, dollars. It's given that after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"4\"><mn>4</mn></math> months, any remaining books are discounted at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"70 percent sign\"><mn>70</mn><mo>%</mo></math> off the consumer price. Thus, the discount amount is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 0.7 right parenthesis left parenthesis 13.50 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mn>0.7</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>13.50</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"9.45\"><mn>9.45</mn></math>, dollars. Subtracting the discount amount from the consumer price gives the discounted price of each of the remaining books: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"13.50 minus 9.45 equals 4.05\"><mn>13.50</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>9.45</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>4.05</mn></math>. Note that 4.05 and 81/20 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1755117017716,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"623dbebb","skill_cd":"Q.B.","score_band_range_cd":6,"skill_desc":"Percentages",